SpeedProfiling (DEV/Football/SpeedProfiling) data gotchas discovered on the BYU G5 2024 game (ticket 05, 2026-07-18):

- Catapult activity venue metadata reports the **practice field** ("Defensive Field (Practice)"), not McLane Stadium — sensor X/Y are venue coords ~600 m off-stadium at unknown rotation, so venue metadata **cannot** drive the venue→field transform. `calibrate_field_transform()` in `R/functions.R` calibrates empirically (PCA on in-play moving samples).
- Field-boundary rule alone counts ~46 athletes/play (team box straddles the calibrated sideline). Participation is boundary rule AND mean metabolic power ≥ 3 W/kg (`PARTICIPATION_MIN_MEAN_M`) → ~10/play. This is a documented method deviation from Alex Carnall's workshop pending Eric's sign-off (PR #3).
- cfbfastR `wallclock` is real per-play UTC and drives alignment, but glitches (4 midnight-UTC plays in the BYU game) — guarded by `WALLCLOCK_MAX_DEV_SEC`. BYU G5 confirmed offset: `TIMESTAMP_OFFSET_SEC <- -15` (wallclock runs ~15 s late vs. GPS; Eric visually confirmed 2026-07-20). `detect_sprint_surge()` prints a suggested offset for future games.
- No pandoc on Eric's machine outside RStudio's bundled copy — use `htmltools::save_html()` / `saveWidget(selfcontained = FALSE)` in R scripts, never `flextable::save_as_html()`.
- v1 (spec `.scratch/speedprofiling/`) closed 2026-07-21, all merged to SpeedProfiling `main`.

Hierarchical milestone (spec `.scratch/speedprofiling-hierarchical/`, PR #6, 2026-07-21):
- **`nlme` model tractability is the whole ballgame on real data.** The 246-sprint BYU game: a nonlinear mixed model with a **sprint-level random effect** (246 tiny groups) and/or **correlated REs** (`~ VHmax + K ~ 1 | position/athlete_id/sprint_id`) does NOT converge — a single fit runs >10 min. **Diagonal REs (`nlme::pdDiag`) with position/athlete grouping and NO sprint-level RE fits in 0.4s.** This is a real spec deviation (drops per-sprint effort modelling) awaiting Eric's sign-off on PR #6. All 246 sprints still feed the fit as replicate rows.
- Plan-author gotchas nlme punished: the double-tilde random formula `~ VHmax + K ~ 1 | g` mis-parses in R (pass a bare grouped formula or a `list(g = pdDiag(...))`), and **`nlme::coef` does not exist** — `coef` is a stats generic, call it bare as `coef(fit, level = n)`.
- Verdict on real game: hierarchical wins held-out RMSE 1.548 vs 1.572 mph but only slightly; `understates-top-speed` flag 16/42 → 12/42. The win is real but concentrated in linemen (DL/TE/OL) — held-out RMSE averages over skill players with long runs where both fits agree, so the aggregate gain looks small.
- This milestone's PRs are NOT stacked (v1's #1→#2→#3 were); hierarchical is a single branch `hierarchical-fit` off `main`.
- **The (VHmax, K) parameterization is ill-conditioned, and that explains everything above.** Fitted VHmax and K correlate r=+0.657 overall and **+0.625 within position** (p=0.0004) — so the shipped diagonal REs violate their own independence assumption (documented, Eric-confirmed 2026-07-22; not distorting output — v1 r=0.625 → hier r=0.703, no attenuation). Reparameterizing to **a₀ = VHmax/K drops it to r=+0.156 (p=0.32)**, i.e. near-total decorrelation. a₀ = initial slope of the velocity–distance curve, dV/dd at d=0, units **s⁻¹** (median 0.98, range 0.44–1.40) — NOT m/s².
- **"Faster athletes accelerate over longer distances" is substantially an estimation artifact, not physiology** — reparameterizing dissolves it, so do NOT assert it publicly without controlled-distance data.
- **Reparameterization was TESTED AND REJECTED 2026-07-22 — do not revisit.** It does NOT recover the sprint-level RE (still fails to converge, 32s/149s: the binding constraint is data sparsity, median 3 sprints/athlete, not conditioning). And it DOES let correlated REs converge (5.1s) — but held-out CV says fixing the independence assumption is *worse*: 1.5621 vs the shipped diagonal's 1.5476 mph. The covariance parameter costs df without buying accuracy. **The diagonal model's independence assumption is a tested simplification, not an outstanding defect.**

Multi-game milestone (spec `.scratch/speedprofiling-multigame/`, PR #7, resolved 2026-07-22, suite 323):
- `assert_single_game()` is **gone**; offset, play windows, and field calibration are all per-`activity_id` now. Offset lives in `GAMES$timestamp_offset_sec` (BYU = −15), not a global.
- **Four** cross-game failure modes existed, two of them found late and both silent: (3) `build_play_windows()` derives play_end from the *next* play and filters on deviation from the **median** wallclock — pooled across fixtures it spans windows across games AND can drop every play in both; (4) `tag_rows_with_plays()` matches **purely on time**, so overlapping cross-game windows silently tag a game-A row with game-B's play. No. 4 is guarded by `assert_no_cross_game_window_overlap()`, not fixed — time-based tagging is only safe while games don't overlap. **Key the tag properly before any same-day double-header.**
- Deferred: STEP 5's kickoff `stop()` fires before STEP 6's saves (a bad pbp pull discards a completed merge); one thin game hard-stops a whole season run (calibration errors below 100 pts).

Profiling rules (spec `.scratch/speedprofiling-rules/`, PR #8, resolved 2026-07-22, suite 365):
- **Eric's 80%-of-peak effort rule is the single biggest accuracy win of the project** — bigger than any model change. `select_profile_sprint()` now takes the longest sprint among those reaching ≥`PROFILE_MIN_EFFORT_FRAC` (0.80) of the athlete's own peak sprint velocity. Held-out RMSE −0.281 mph mean (~14%), Wilcoxon p=6.21e-05, 25 improved/10 worsened, `understates-top-speed` 16/42 → 11/41, zero fallbacks needed. **Lesson: ask the domain expert before modelling harder.**
- Peak means the athlete's own max across their qualifying sprints — NOT observed game max (which includes non-sprint moments and sets an unreachable bar).
- **Do NOT apply the effort filter to the hierarchical fit's input** — measured worse (+0.024 mph). It starves the pooled model of the short-run sprints linemen borrow structure from. The two mechanisms are complementary: max-effort selection for the single-sprint NLS fit, everything for the pooled model.
- Position pooling now uses `profile_group` (from `POSITION_GROUP_MAP`), not raw `position`: S+STAR merged to `S_STAR`, SP excluded via `PROFILE_EXCLUDE_POSITIONS`. `position` is never overwritten. **This merge measured as neutral (−0.0010 mph) — it ships because it matches the roster's meaning, NOT as an accuracy win. Do not present it as one.**
- Eric's standing answers (2026-07-22): keep Alex's workshop velocity zones (no Baylor bands); linemen testing day deferred; `REVIEW_*` thresholds are the agent's call; season is one practice/day + one game/week and camp sessions have no max-velocity attempts, so the time-based play-tagging overlap guard is sufficient.
- ~~Blocked on Eric: no credentials~~ RESOLVED: `CATAPULT_TOKEN` / `CFBD_API_KEY` ARE present at `C:\Users\eric_rash\Documents\.Renviron` — they only look missing when R runs under Git Bash (its `HOME` override points R at the wrong `.Renviron`). Run R from PowerShell.

TCU full-roster correction (branch `tcu-fix-pull-retry`, PR #9, 2026-07-23):
- **"TCU degraded game" retracted.** The 30-of-66 athlete count was script 01 silently dropping athletes on the FIRST transient API error during the long sequential pull. Retry per athlete (`PULL_MAX_ATTEMPTS` 4, `PULL_RETRY_SLEEP_S` 2) recovered **66/66, 0 permanent failures**. Genuine empty pulls (NULL, no error) are not retried.
- Corrected 3-game numbers: **503 sprints / 73 athletes** (TCU 155 vs 58 half-dropped), **71 profiled**, **32 well-identified ≥20 yd**, hier held-out RMSE **1.393** (v1 1.414), understated 14. Offset −15 participation-confirmed on full roster (sweep peaks −15, 10.58 athl/play vs BYU 10.75); TCU's surge SCAN is unreliable — locks onto a pregame-warmup false cluster (~−152).
- Every pipeline `.rds` write is now `save_rds_atomic()` (config.R: per-PID `.tmp` + rename). Torn files can no longer be left by kills or overlapping runs.
- **Run scripts 01–03 one at a time.** The 15.6 GB machine fits exactly one run (each 02/03 peaks ~9 GB); concurrent runs OOM-kill each other **silently** (process vanishes, no R error in the log) — that, not interleaved writes, is what tore `data_tagged.rds` twice on 2026-07-23. Check `Get-Process Rscript` before launching.
- Long R runs: launch **detached** via `Start-Process Rscript -RedirectStandardOutput/-Error` — child-process background jobs get killed ~10 min. R's `message()` goes to stderr.
- PowerShell gotcha: `Rscript -e "..."` in a double-quoted string interpolates R's `$column` as (empty) PS variables, silently corrupting the code — always write R to a script file.
- Parallel-session hazard: two Claude sessions executed the same handoff concurrently (racing R runs, config edits, and the commit). Before redoing handoff work, check `git log`, `gh pr list`, and running processes — the other session may already have shipped it.

2025 season scale-up (Auburn PR #10 merged, SMU PR #11, 2026-07-25/26):
- **Offsets are PER GAME, not systematic to the provider** — Auburn G1 is -25 (Eric GIF-confirmed), breaking the -15 streak. Inspect every new game.
- **KICKOFF wallclock stamps individually lag 0–3.5 min** (stat crew logs the kickoff late while entering the preceding score; measured -14..-216 s across SMU's 11 kickoffs). The surge scan/animation chases these bad stamps — SMU suggested -191 then -371, both wrong (game offset is -15). **TCU's "-152 false warmup cluster" was actually this** — correct the earlier claim. Anchor alignment on the OPENING kickoff or the HALFTIME LULL (GPS quiet begins ≈ last-Q2-play stamp + play duration); never a mid-game kickoff.
- The participation-sweep proxy (M≥3, no boundary rule) discriminates offsets WEAKLY (~0.2 athletes/play margins) — treat as corroboration, not proof. The halftime anchor is the sharp instrument. Probe scripts: `~/.claude/jobs/010026e9/tmp/smu_halftime_probe.R`, `smu_kickoff_lag_probe.R` (promote into repo if needed again).
- 2025 pbp_week map (cfbd-confirmed): byes at weeks 7 & 11 → G1-G6 = wk 1-6, G7-G9 = wk 8-10, G10-G12 = wk 12-14.
- Five-game state: 1074 sprints / 88 profiled / 45 well-identified; flag system caught a 41-mph single-sprint NLS blowup (hier estimate sane at 18.6).
- Machine sleep freezes detached R runs (process survives but thrashes on wake) — kill and relaunch rather than wait. The MSYS keep-alive task can die on sleep/wake; it now auto-restarts (999×/1 min).
- **Pull completeness is now GATED, not just retried** (PR #9, 2026-07-23). Retry alone still let a pull proceed with a silent gap if every attempt failed for an athlete. `pull_game_10hz()` now names any athlete returning no data and **refuses to cache** a game missing >`PULL_MIN_ROSTER_FRAC` (0.90) of its Catapult roster — a failed pull errors and is re-run instead of caching half a game. Verified: broken TCU 30/66 (45%) would STOP; 61/61, 66/66, and even 65/66 (one genuinely unmiked athlete) proceed.
- **Audited 2026-07-23: all three cached games match their live Catapult rosters exactly** (BYU 61/61, Texas Tech 61/61, TCU 66/66). BYU and Texas Tech were pulled with the old no-retry code but were never truncated, so every pre-fix milestone number stands. Re-run this audit (roster vs `n_distinct(athlete_id)` per cache) after any future pull.
